(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-2-cardiac-biomarkers-lipids-quiz"]={"title":"Cardiac Biomarkers & Lipids Quiz 1 — Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 59-year-old on high-intensity therapy has triglycerides of 190 mg/dL (desirable below 150 mg/dL) and a low LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol). Which LDL-C estimate is preferred?","choices":["The Friedewald equation alone","Total cholesterol minus triglycerides","Martin/Hopkins or Sampson/NIH equation","HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol) doubled"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They are preferred for greater accuracy, especially when triglycerides are 150 mg/dL or above or LDL-C is low, which is this patient. NIH is the National Institutes of Health. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 61-year-old has a low HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol), below her lab's reference limit. How is that value used?","choices":["As the main target of therapy","As a marker, not a target","As proof of familial disease","As a value to ignore"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Higher HDL-C is generally favorable, but low HDL-C is a marker, not a therapeutic goal; treatment is aimed at the apoB (apolipoprotein B) particles. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 58-year-old's high-sensitivity C-reactive protein is repeatedly above the high-risk tier. What does a persistently high level independently predict?","choices":["An acute infarction within hours","Skeletal muscle breakdown","Heart failure and cardiac mortality","A falsely low troponin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High levels are an independent risk factor for heart failure and cardiac mortality, and the level is directly proportional to cardiovascular risk. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 67-year-old on hemodialysis with no chest pain has two troponin values just above the 99th percentile limit, unchanged between draws. What does this pattern represent?","choices":["Chronic myocardial injury","Acute type 1 infarction","A falsely negative result","Acute heart failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Troponin can be chronically elevated in chronic kidney disease, heart failure and structural heart disease. Without ischemia and a rising or falling pattern, this is not infarction. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 55-year-old's lipid values are entered into a cardiovascular risk equation. What does that step produce?","choices":["A diagnosis of acute infarction","A 10-year risk estimate","A measured LDL-C value","A lifetime lipoprotein(a) level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lipid values feed 10-year risk estimation, with the PREVENT (Predicting Risk of cardiovascular disease EVENTs) equations in the current guideline, and the estimate then drives treatment intensity. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man's chart lists a high-sensitivity troponin. What does the term high-sensitivity describe?","choices":["A fourth troponin subunit","A skeletal muscle troponin","A troponin corrected for age","The assay's characteristics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is not a different subunit of troponin. The assay detects circulating troponin at lower concentrations, which is where its advantage comes from. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 55-year-old asks how LDL (low-density lipoprotein) forms in the circulation. What is the sequence?","choices":["HDL gains triglyceride, becoming a remnant, then LDL","Chylomicrons gain cholesterol in the liver, then LDL","VLDL loses triglyceride, becoming a remnant, then LDL","LDL loses cholesterol, becoming a remnant, then VLDL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The liver secretes triglyceride-rich VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein); lipoprotein lipase strips triglyceride to give a remnant (IDL, intermediate-density lipoprotein) and then cholesterol-rich LDL. It is the same apolipoprotein B particle at different stages. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 48-year-old asks why cholesterol travels in the blood inside particles. What is the reason?","choices":["Cholesterol is broken down by plasma enzymes","Particles let the kidney filter cholesterol","Free cholesterol would raise the troponin","Cholesterol and triglycerides are insoluble"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The major lipids, cholesterol and triglycerides, are insoluble in plasma, so they travel packaged inside lipoproteins. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 42-year-old eats a fatty meal and chylomicrons appear in her plasma. Which apolipoprotein do they carry?","choices":["ApoB-48 (apolipoprotein B-48), made by the intestine","ApoB-100 (apolipoprotein B-100), made by the liver","ApoA-I (apolipoprotein A-I), made by the liver","Apo(a) (apolipoprotein(a)), made by the liver"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. ApoB-48 is synthesized by the intestines and found in chylomicrons, which carry dietary triglyceride. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man started a statin two months ago and now has diffuse muscle aches. Which biomarker is the appropriate test?","choices":["High-sensitivity troponin","Creatine kinase","B-type natriuretic peptide","High-sensitivity C-reactive protein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Statin myopathy and rhabdomyolysis are skeletal muscle questions, and creatine kinase is the right test for them. Troponin is not. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 58-year-old's 10-year PREVENT (Predicting Risk of cardiovascular disease EVENTs) estimate is 7%, in the intermediate tier of 5% to under 10%, and she is unsure about starting therapy. What may be considered to reclassify her risk?","choices":["A high-sensitivity troponin","A B-type natriuretic peptide","A creatine kinase","A coronary artery calcium score"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In the Calculate, Personalize, Reclassify framework, when there is clinical or patient uncertainty at borderline or intermediate risk, a coronary artery calcium score is considered to revise the estimate. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 64-year-old has an acute coronary syndrome confirmed. Besides troponin, which biomarker also carries prognostic value after the event?","choices":["Creatine kinase","Myoglobin","Lactate dehydrogenase","B-type natriuretic peptide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. B-type natriuretic peptide or its N-terminal fragment supports heart failure diagnosis and triage and is also prognostic after acute coronary syndrome. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 39-year-old is found to have a variant of an apolipoprotein that is critical in triglyceride clearance. Which apolipoprotein is that?","choices":["ApoA-II (apolipoprotein A-II)","ApoE (apolipoprotein E)","ApoB-48 (apolipoprotein B-48)","Apo(a) (apolipoprotein(a))"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. ApoE is important in triglyceride metabolism and critical in triglyceride clearance. ApoC, apoC-III and apoA-V regulate triglyceride metabolism. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 50-year-old in primary prevention has borderline cardiovascular risk. When is high-sensitivity C-reactive protein appropriate?","choices":["To diagnose acute infarction","To triage acute dyspnea","To refine atherosclerotic risk","To detect statin myopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is an inflammatory risk-enhancing marker in select primary-prevention patients, not a diagnostic test for an acute cardiac event. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 57-year-old's clinician chooses to follow non-HDL-C (non-high-density lipoprotein cholesterol) rather than LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol). Why?","choices":["It is measured directly by the lab","It excludes remnant particles","It has a single normal value","It predicts risk better, at no cost"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It captures cholesterol in every atherogenic apoB (apolipoprotein B) lipoprotein, adds no cost, predicts atherosclerotic risk better than LDL-C, and is recommended for routine reporting. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man's troponin report says his value exceeds the upper reference limit. What does that limit represent?","choices":["A value higher than 99% of all cardiac patients","A value higher than 99% of a healthy population","The level at which every patient has an infarction","The value that proves the troponin is from skeletal muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A value above it indicates some level of cardiac muscle damage and signals the need for further diagnostic testing or intervention. Sex-specific limits are generally agreed on. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 60-year-old asks why VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) remnants and LDL (low-density lipoprotein) all count toward plaque. What do they share?","choices":["Each is an apoB particle small enough to enter the artery wall","Each carries apoA-I and removes cholesterol from the wall","Each is made in the intestine from dietary fat after meals","Each is too large to cross the endothelium into the wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each apoB (apolipoprotein B) particle under about 70 nm can cross the endothelium and become trapped in the artery wall, initiating plaque. That is why therapy aims to reduce the number of circulating apoB particles. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 71-year-old has troponin monitored after coronary artery bypass grafting. Which rise defines a surgery-related (type 5) infarction?","choices":["More than 5 times the 99th percentile limit","Any value above the 99th percentile limit","More than 10 times the 99th percentile limit","More than 2 times the 99th percentile limit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Coronary artery bypass graft-related infarction, type 5, needs more than 10 times the 99th percentile upper reference limit, a higher bar than for percutaneous intervention. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has had crushing chest pain with diaphoresis for 40 minutes. Which biomarker testing is first-line?","choices":["A single creatine kinase level","Serial high-sensitivity troponin","Serial myoglobin levels","High-sensitivity C-reactive protein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Serial values are the point: a rising or falling pattern identifies acute injury, and the initial troponin also carries prognostic weight in acute coronary syndrome. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with rest angina has serial troponins that stay below the 99th percentile limit. What do the results indicate?","choices":["A small infarction has occurred","The test is invalid in angina","Heart failure is excluded","No cardiac muscle injury has occurred"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Normal troponins in unstable angina mean no myocyte injury. Elevated troponins would mean injury, which stratifies risk and prompts consideration of revascularization. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 69-year-old with new exertional dyspnea has a B-type natriuretic peptide below the assay's heart failure cutoff. How is that result used?","choices":["To rule out new-onset heart failure","To rule in acute myocardial infarction","To confirm heart failure on its own","To estimate long-term atherosclerotic risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The natriuretic peptides can serve as a rule-out test for suspected new-onset heart failure, used together with the clinical assessment and an echocardiogram if indicated. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man is found to have a markedly elevated lipoprotein(a) after a premature myocardial infarction. Whom should testing be extended to?","choices":["His first-degree relatives","No one; it is not inherited","Only his spouse","Only relatives over 65"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Testing is indicated in those with a personal or family history of atherosclerotic disease, with cascade testing in appropriate families, because the level is more than 90% genetically determined. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 55-year-old presents two days after an episode of chest pain. Why is troponin preferred over CK-MB (creatine kinase muscle-brain isoenzyme)?","choices":["It rises later and clears more quickly","It is also raised by skeletal muscle injury","It rises sooner and stays elevated longer","It is cheaper and needs no clinical context"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That expands the window for diagnosis and treatment, which matters for a patient presenting late. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 53-year-old's report shows lipoprotein(a) and apoB (apolipoprotein B) on the same panel. Why are they reported together?","choices":["Lipoprotein(a) adds to the apoB count","ApoB is made from lipoprotein(a)","Both are acute-phase reactants","Both are fixed genetic values"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each lipoprotein(a) particle carries one apoB-100 (apolipoprotein B-100), so it contributes to the total apoB particle count. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 3-year-old's father had a myocardial infarction at 38 and has familial hypercholesterolemia. When may the child be screened?","choices":["Now, from age 2","At age 9 to 11","At age 19","Only if symptoms appear"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Screening at age 2 or older is recommended with a family history of premature atherosclerotic disease, severe hypercholesterolemia or familial hypercholesterolemia. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 56-year-old's LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol) is at goal, but his triglycerides stay above the desirable level of 150 mg/dL. What do his triglyceride-rich lipoproteins contribute?","choices":["Residual atherosclerotic risk","Protection from atherosclerosis","No risk once LDL-C is at goal","A falsely low apoB level"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Remnant cholesterol and triglyceride-rich lipoproteins contribute residual risk; VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) remnants and IDL (intermediate-density lipoprotein) are atherogenic. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 49-year-old has triglycerides of 180 mg/dL (desirable below 150 mg/dL) and no other lipid abnormality. How is this triglyceride level classed?","choices":["Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Normal, with no significance","Diagnostic of familial hypercholesterolemia","A risk enhancer for atherosclerotic disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Triglycerides of 150 mg/dL or above mean increased atherosclerotic risk and act as a risk enhancer; severe hypertriglyceridemia begins at 500 mg/dL. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 44-year-old with a strong family history has lipoprotein(a) measured. What is lipoprotein(a)?","choices":["An HDL-like particle with apo(a) bound to apoA-I","An LDL-like particle with apo(a) bound to apoB-100","A chylomicron remnant that carries apoB-48","A free apolipoprotein that circulates alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lipoprotein(a) is an LDL (low-density lipoprotein)-like particle in which apo(a) (apolipoprotein(a)) is bound to apoB-100 (apolipoprotein B-100). It is more atherogenic than LDL. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 50-year-old's lipid report mentions lipoproteins. What makes up a lipoprotein?","choices":["A protein core wrapped in a triglyceride shell","A lipid core, phospholipid shell and apolipoproteins","Free fatty acids bound to albumin alone","Cholesterol crystals coated with fibrin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The core holds cholesterol esters and triglycerides; the shell is phospholipid; the apolipoproteins are the structural and regulatory proteins on its surface. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 50-year-old with a body mass index of 44 has orthopnea and edema, yet a B-type natriuretic peptide below the heart failure cutoff. What explains the discrepancy?","choices":["Obesity falsely raises natriuretic peptides","Obesity makes troponin falsely positive","Obesity falsely lowers natriuretic peptides","Obesity makes the assay invalid entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A high body mass index is associated with falsely low natriuretic peptide levels, so a normal-looking value does not reassure in this patient. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 15"}]};
